# D120 Batch 1: Menopause (15) + Contraception (15) + Prenatal (20) = 50 questions
questions = [
  # ===== MENOPAUSE (15) =====
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"A 51-year-old woman has had no menstrual period for 14 months, hot flashes, vaginal dryness, and difficulty sleeping. She has no contraindications to hormone therapy. What is the most effective treatment for vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes)?","a":["Black cohosh supplement","Systemic hormone therapy (estrogen + progestogen for women with intact uterus) — most effective treatment for vasomotor symptoms","Low-dose SSRIs alone","Vaginal estrogen only"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Systemic hormone therapy (HT) is the gold-standard, most effective treatment for moderate-to-severe menopausal vasomotor symptoms. Women with an INTACT uterus must receive combined estrogen + progestogen (to prevent endometrial hyperplasia/cancer from unopposed estrogen). Women post-hysterectomy: estrogen alone. **Board tip:** The WHI study (2002) caused HT decline, but reanalysis shows HT benefit/risk ratio is favorable in healthy women <60 or within 10 years of menopause ('timing hypothesis'). For women >60 or >10 years post-menopause, HT risks (CVD, clot, stroke) increase."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"Which condition is an ABSOLUTE CONTRAINDICATION to systemic hormone therapy in a menopausal woman?","a":["Mild osteoporosis","Personal history of breast cancer, history of DVT/PE, active liver disease, or unexplained vaginal bleeding","Mild hypertension controlled on medication","Age over 60 years"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Absolute contraindications to systemic HT: personal history of breast cancer (especially ER+ cancer), history of DVT/PE or thrombophilic condition, active liver disease, unexplained vaginal bleeding (must evaluate first), coronary artery disease, recent MI or stroke. **Board tip:** HT is NOT absolutely contraindicated just because of age, but risk-benefit must be reassessed. For women with vasomotor symptoms AND contraindications to HT, alternatives include: paroxetine (FDA-approved for hot flashes), venlafaxine, gabapentin, or oxybutynin."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"A 54-year-old woman is concerned about genitourinary syndrome of menopause (GSM) — vaginal dryness, dyspareunia, and urinary urgency. She does not want systemic therapy. What is first-line treatment?","a":["Systemic oral estrogen only","Vaginal lubricants and moisturizers (non-hormonal first-line) ± low-dose vaginal estrogen (cream, ring, tablet) for moderate-severe symptoms","Vaginal dilators only","Oral SSRIs"],"correct":1,"rationale":"GSM management: Non-hormonal first: water-based or silicone-based lubricants (for intercourse) and vaginal moisturizers (regular use for dryness). For moderate-severe symptoms not responding to non-hormonal measures: low-dose topical vaginal estrogen (Estring, Vagifem, Premarin cream) — minimal systemic absorption, safe for most women including those with breast cancer history (discuss with oncologist). **Board tip:** Ospemifene (Osphena) is an oral selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) approved for GSM with dyspareunia. Prasterone (Intrarosa) is a vaginal DHEA also approved for dyspareunia."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"A 56-year-old woman had her last period 3 years ago and wishes to start hormone therapy for hot flashes. She had a hysterectomy at age 42. What HT regimen is appropriate?","a":["Estrogen + progestogen (combined therapy)","Estrogen-only therapy — progestogen is only needed for uterine protection in women with intact uterus","Progestogen-only therapy","No hormone therapy ever — 3 years post-menopause is too late"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Women post-hysterectomy (no uterus) receive ESTROGEN ONLY — progestogen is not needed because there is no endometrium to protect. Adding progestogen unnecessarily increases breast cancer risk. Women with intact uterus MUST have progestogen added to prevent endometrial hyperplasia. **Board tip:** Route of administration: transdermal estrogen has lower VTE risk than oral estrogen (avoids first-pass hepatic effect). Micronized progesterone (Prometrium) has more favorable profile than synthetic progestins for breast cancer risk."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"What non-hormonal FDA-approved medication was specifically approved in 2023 for moderate-to-severe vasomotor symptoms associated with menopause?","a":["Paroxetine (Brisdelle) 7.5 mg","Fezolinetant (Veozah) — neurokinin B receptor antagonist","Gabapentin","Clonidine"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Fezolinetant (Veozah) received FDA approval in May 2023 — the first non-hormonal, non-SSRI/SNRI medication specifically targeting the NK3 receptor pathway that mediates vasomotor symptoms. It blocks neurokinin B (NKB) signaling in the hypothalamic thermoregulatory center. **Board tip:** The NK3 receptor pathway is the mechanism behind hot flashes — estrogen normally suppresses NKB. Paroxetine 7.5 mg (Brisdelle) was the first FDA-approved non-hormonal for hot flashes (2013). Both are options for women with contraindications to HT."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"How is menopause officially defined clinically?","a":["First missed period after age 45","12 consecutive months of amenorrhea in the absence of other causes, after the final menstrual period","FSH level >30 mIU/mL","Onset of vasomotor symptoms with any irregular periods"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Menopause is defined as 12 consecutive months of amenorrhea after the final menstrual period (FMP), in the absence of other pathological or physiological causes. It is a retrospective diagnosis — the FMP is only confirmed after 12 months of amenorrhea. Average age: 51.3 years in the U.S. **Board tip:** Premature ovarian insufficiency (POI): menopause-like symptoms before age 40 — requires investigation (chromosome analysis, autoimmune workup). Elevated FSH (>40 mIU/mL) + low estradiol confirm POI. Women with POI should generally receive HT until age 51."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"A 52-year-old perimenopausal woman on combined estrogen-progestogen HT reports unexpected vaginal bleeding after 6 months on therapy. What is the appropriate next step?","a":["Reassure — breakthrough bleeding is expected on HT","Endometrial evaluation with transvaginal ultrasound and/or endometrial biopsy to rule out endometrial hyperplasia or cancer","Switch HT regimen only — no further workup","Stop HT immediately, no workup needed"],"correct":1,"rationale":"ANY unexpected vaginal bleeding on HT requires evaluation to rule out endometrial pathology. Workup: transvaginal ultrasound (endometrial stripe <4mm = low risk) AND/OR endometrial biopsy. Bleeding after 12 months of amenorrhea (postmenopausal bleeding) ALWAYS requires evaluation regardless of HT use. **Board tip:** Postmenopausal bleeding: rule out endometrial cancer. Most common benign cause: endometrial atrophy or polyps. TVS endometrial thickness ≥4mm in postmenopausal woman → endometrial biopsy. This is a critical gynecologic red flag."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"Hormone therapy is known to affect bone density. What is the role of HT in osteoporosis prevention?","a":["HT increases fracture risk — never use for bone health","HT preserves bone density and reduces fracture risk, but is not first-line for osteoporosis treatment (bisphosphonates are); appropriate to consider in younger menopausal women with vasomotor symptoms AND bone concerns","HT has no effect on bone density","HT is more effective than bisphosphonates for established osteoporosis"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Estrogen maintains bone density by inhibiting osteoclast activity. HT significantly reduces vertebral and hip fracture risk. However, for osteoporosis TREATMENT, bisphosphonates (alendronate, risedronate) or denosumab are first-line. HT is appropriate for women taking it for vasomotor symptoms who also have bone density concerns. **Board tip:** When HT is stopped, bone density returns to pre-treatment levels. DEXA scan is recommended at age 65 (or earlier if fracture risk factors). FRAX tool assesses 10-year fracture risk."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"A 50-year-old woman with depression and hot flashes asks about alternatives to HT. Which SSRI has an FDA indication specifically for menopause-related vasomotor symptoms?","a":["Sertraline (Zoloft)","Paroxetine mesylate 7.5 mg (Brisdelle) — the only FDA-approved non-hormonal for hot flashes (as of 2023, fezolinetant also approved)","Fluoxetine","Escitalopram"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Paroxetine mesylate 7.5 mg (Brisdelle) was FDA-approved in 2013 for moderate-to-severe vasomotor symptoms of menopause. Off-label options with evidence: venlafaxine, escitalopram, citalopram, desvenlafaxine (not as effective as HT but useful for women with contraindications). **Board tip:** Avoid paroxetine in women on tamoxifen (paroxetine inhibits CYP2D6, which converts tamoxifen to its active metabolite endoxifen — reduces tamoxifen efficacy). Use venlafaxine or citalopram instead for hot flashes in tamoxifen users."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"During perimenopause, a 47-year-old woman with irregular periods wants contraception. She asks if she still needs to use birth control. What is the appropriate counseling?","a":["No — periods are irregular, so she cannot get pregnant","Contraception is needed until 12 consecutive months of amenorrhea (menopause) — irregular cycles do not mean infertility","Contraception is only needed if FSH is <30","Menopause begins at age 45, so contraception is no longer needed"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Perimenopausal women can still ovulate intermittently despite irregular cycles — pregnancy is possible until menopause (12 months of amenorrhea). Reliable contraception should continue until menopause is confirmed. Low-dose combined OCP is both contraceptive AND treats perimenopausal symptoms. **Board tip:** Perimenopause = transition phase with irregular cycles, vasomotor symptoms, anovulatory cycles. Can last 4-10 years. Combined OCP can be used until age 50-55 in healthy non-smoking women to control symptoms and provide contraception."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"Which symptom of menopause has the HIGHEST impact on quality of life according to patient surveys?","a":["Vaginal dryness","Hot flashes/vasomotor symptoms — affect up to 80% of women, can be severely disruptive to sleep and daily functioning","Joint pain","Hair thinning"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes and night sweats) are the most bothersome and prevalent symptoms of menopause, affecting ~75-80% of women. Night sweats cause significant sleep disruption, which compounds cognitive effects ('brain fog'), mood changes, and fatigue. **Board tip:** Symptom duration varies: average 7.4 years from perimenopause through post-menopause (longer in Black women who have more severe, longer-lasting hot flashes — disparities in menopause care are clinically important)."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"A 58-year-old woman started HT at age 50 for vasomotor symptoms and is now asking whether to continue. She has no symptoms. What is the current guidance on HT duration?","a":["All women must stop HT at age 55","Continue indefinitely — no reason to stop if tolerated","Reassess annually — there is no arbitrary limit; continue at lowest effective dose for symptom control, with regular review of risks and benefits","Stop after exactly 5 years always"],"correct":2,"rationale":"There is no mandatory stopping point for HT. The Menopause Society (formerly NAMS) recommends annual risk-benefit reassessment. Some women need HT into their 60s or beyond for symptom control or bone protection (with careful assessment of cardiovascular and breast cancer risk). **Board tip:** When stopping HT, taper gradually to reduce symptom recurrence. Women who restart HT after long gaps are at higher cardiovascular risk than those who use it continuously in early menopause."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"What screening is most important for women on long-term hormone therapy?","a":["Annual colonoscopy","Annual mammogram, biennial DEXA scan, regular Pap/co-testing per guidelines, BP monitoring, and clinical breast exam","Monthly vaginal ultrasound","Annual brain MRI"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Women on HT require: annual mammogram (HT, especially combined, slightly increases breast cancer density and risk with prolonged use), regular Pap/HPV co-testing per guidelines, BP monitoring (estrogen can mildly increase BP), DEXA scan per osteoporosis screening guidelines. **Board tip:** HT slightly increases breast cancer risk with >3-5 years of combined estrogen-progestogen use. Estrogen-only HT (post-hysterectomy) has a more favorable breast cancer profile — may even have a slight protective effect. This nuance is board-relevant."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"A 53-year-old menopausal woman reports new-onset depressive symptoms. She denies prior psychiatric history. What should be considered as a contributing factor?","a":["Menopause has no effect on mood — refer to psychiatry only","Perimenopause and early postmenopause are periods of increased vulnerability for new-onset depression due to estrogen fluctuations — assess for MDD and consider both antidepressant therapy AND HT evaluation","Depressive symptoms in menopause indicate bipolar disorder","This is normal aging — no treatment warranted"],"correct":1,"rationale":"The perimenopause transition is associated with a 2-4x increased risk of new-onset depression, even in women without prior psychiatric history. Estrogen has significant effects on serotonin and dopamine systems. HT may improve mood in perimenopausal women, but antidepressants remain the primary treatment for MDD. **Board tip:** Screen for depression at all menopausal visits (PHQ-9). Distinguish between: depressive disorder (treat with antidepressant ± therapy) vs. mood changes from sleep disruption from vasomotor symptoms (treat the hot flashes → improved sleep → improved mood)."},
  {"topic":"Menopause","course":"D120","q":"Which non-pharmacological interventions have the best evidence for reducing hot flash frequency and severity?","a":["Soy isoflavones and red clover — proven equivalent to HT","Weight loss (if overweight/obese), avoiding hot flash triggers (alcohol, caffeine, spicy foods, hot environments), cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT), and clinical hypnosis","Cold showers and fan use only","Vitamin E supplements — strong evidence base"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Non-pharmacological interventions with best evidence: CBT (reduces hot flash distress, well-studied), clinical hypnosis (reduces frequency), and lifestyle modifications (weight loss, trigger avoidance). Cooling techniques (fans, layered clothing, cooling pillows) provide symptomatic relief. **Board tip:** Soy isoflavones and phytoestrogens: weak, inconsistent evidence — not recommended as primary treatment. Mind-body interventions (CBT, hypnosis) may be preferable for women who want to avoid all medications or hormones."},

  # ===== CONTRACEPTION (15) =====
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A 22-year-old nulliparous woman wants the most effective reversible contraception. She is not currently in a relationship and wants 'set it and forget it' contraception. What should you offer first?","a":["Combined oral contraceptive pills","Long-acting reversible contraception (LARC) — IUD (levonorgestrel or copper) or subdermal implant — >99% effective, most effective reversible contraception available","Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA) injection","Barrier methods"],"correct":1,"rationale":"LARC (Long-Acting Reversible Contraception) methods — hormonal IUDs (Mirena, Kyleena, Liletta, Skyla), copper IUD (Paragard), and subdermal implant (Nexplanon) — are the most effective reversible contraception (>99%), require no daily action, and are recommended as first-line by ACOG for all women including adolescents and nulliparous women. **Board tip:** Nulliparity is NOT a contraindication to IUD insertion. Effectiveness: implant=IUD >injection >combined OCP. The 'unintended pregnancy gap' is largely explained by user error with pills — LARCs eliminate this."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A 28-year-old woman requests the copper IUD (Paragard). What is a UNIQUE advantage of the copper IUD compared to hormonal IUDs?","a":["It reduces menstrual bleeding","It can be used as emergency contraception if inserted within 5 days of unprotected intercourse — most effective EC available (>99%)","It protects against STIs","It is effective for 20+ years routinely"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Copper IUD unique advantages: (1) Hormone-free (ideal for women who cannot use hormones), (2) Most effective emergency contraception available when inserted within 5 days of unprotected sex (>99% effective), (3) Approved for 10 years (may work up to 12+ years). **Board tip:** Copper IUD as EC works by copper-mediated spermicidal/toxic effect that also prevents implantation. It is more effective than levonorgestrel EC pill. Disadvantage: heavier, more painful periods (vs. hormonal IUDs which reduce bleeding). Paragard is approved for 10 years but studies show efficacy up to 12 years."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A 32-year-old woman with hypertension (BP 158/95) and BMI of 35 wants oral contraception. Which method is CONTRAINDICATED?","a":["Progestin-only pill (minipill)","Combined oral contraceptive pills (containing estrogen) — contraindicated in uncontrolled hypertension due to increased stroke/MI risk","Levonorgestrel IUD","Progestin-only implant (Nexplanon)"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Combined OCP (estrogen-containing) contraindications include: uncontrolled hypertension (BP ≥160/100), personal history of VTE, stroke, CAD, migraine with aura, active liver disease, >35 years AND smoking ≥15 cigarettes/day, and breastfeeding <6 weeks postpartum. **Board tip:** Use the WHO Medical Eligibility Criteria (MEC) categories 1-4: Category 1 (no restriction) to Category 4 (absolute contraindication). For women with hypertension, progestin-only methods (minipill, IUD, implant, DMPA injection) are MEC Category 1-2 and are safe alternatives."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A 17-year-old asks for emergency contraception after unprotected sex 36 hours ago. She weighs 175 lbs. What is the most effective oral EC option?","a":["Levonorgestrel (Plan B) — equally effective regardless of weight","Ulipristal acetate (ella) — more effective than levonorgestrel, especially in women >165 lbs, up to 120 hours after intercourse","Combined OCP Yuzpe method","No oral EC is effective after 24 hours"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Ulipristal acetate (ella) is more effective than levonorgestrel EC, particularly in women weighing >165 lbs where levonorgestrel efficacy decreases significantly. Ella works up to 120 hours (5 days). Copper IUD insertion (up to 5 days) is more effective than any pill. **Board tip:** EC options in order of effectiveness: Copper IUD > ulipristal acetate (ella) > levonorgestrel (Plan B). Levonorgestrel reduces pregnancy risk by 89% if taken within 72 hours. It does NOT cause abortion — it prevents fertilization/implantation."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A patient on the levonorgestrel-releasing IUD (Mirena) asks about expected changes in her period. What is accurate counseling?","a":["Periods will become heavier and more painful","Spotting and irregular bleeding for 3-6 months initially, then 20-50% of women have NO periods at all by 12 months — this is the intended effect","Periods immediately stop from day 1","No change in menstrual pattern"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Hormonal IUDs (especially Mirena, 52mg LNG): cause endometrial atrophy, leading to significantly lighter periods and eventually amenorrhea in 20-50% of users after 12 months. This is a BENEFIT, not a concern. Initial 3-6 months: irregular spotting/cramping as normal adjustment. **Board tip:** Hormonal IUDs are FDA-indicated for TREATMENT of heavy menstrual bleeding (Mirena) — a significant non-contraceptive benefit. Counsel patients that amenorrhea on hormonal IUD is safe and expected. No need to 'check' with pregnancy tests unless other symptoms present."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A 35-year-old smoker who smokes 20 cigarettes/day asks about combined OCP. What is the appropriate recommendation?","a":["COC is safe in all smokers under age 40","COC is absolutely contraindicated in women ≥35 years who smoke ≥15 cigarettes/day due to greatly increased risk of MI, stroke, and VTE","Reduce smoking to <10/day and OCP is fine","Only patch is contraindicated, not pills"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Smoking ≥15 cigarettes/day + age ≥35 + estrogen = WHO MEC Category 4 (absolute contraindication). Estrogen + nicotine synergistically increase thrombogenic risk, causing arterial and venous thromboembolism. Offer progestin-only methods: minipill, IUD, implant, or DMPA injection. **Board tip:** The cardiovascular risks of combined OCP increase with age (especially ≥35), smoking, hypertension, and migraine with aura. Progestin-only methods are safe for smokers. Strongly recommend smoking cessation counseling at every visit."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"The subdermal contraceptive implant (Nexplanon) is inserted in the inner upper arm. How long is it effective and what is the failure rate?","a":["1 year; 5% failure rate","3 years (up to 5 years with off-label use); <0.1% failure rate — most effective reversible contraceptive available","5 years; 0.5% failure rate","1 year; must be reinserted annually"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Nexplanon (etonogestrel 68mg subdermal implant): FDA-approved for 3 years, with evidence supporting efficacy up to 5 years in non-obese women. Failure rate: 0.05% (5 per 10,000 women over 1 year) — among the most effective methods ever tested. Mechanism: primarily suppresses ovulation. **Board tip:** Nexplanon insertion/removal requires specific training (minor surgical procedure). Irregular bleeding is the most common reason for early discontinuation — counsel beforehand. Obese women may have reduced efficacy near the end of the 3-year period due to higher ENG distribution volume."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate (DMPA/Depo-Provera) injection is given every 3 months. What is the most important LONG-TERM concern with this method?","a":["Permanent infertility","Bone mineral density loss with prolonged use (>2 years) — reversible upon discontinuation; calcium/vitamin D supplementation recommended","Increased risk of breast cancer with every injection","Permanent amenorrhea"],"correct":1,"rationale":"DMPA concern: bone mineral density (BMD) decreases with prolonged use, especially in adolescents still building peak bone mass. The FDA issued a black box warning recommending use >2 years only if other methods are inadequate. BMD recovers after discontinuation. **Board tip:** DMPA fertility return: delayed ovulation return (average 10 months after last injection). Not appropriate for women planning pregnancy in the near future. Benefits: reduced dysmenorrhea, endometriosis pain, sickle cell crises, and seizures. Useful for women with developmental disabilities or those who cannot use estrogen."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A woman with a history of ectopic pregnancy asks if she can use an IUD. What is the correct counseling?","a":["IUD is absolutely contraindicated after ectopic pregnancy","Prior ectopic is NOT a contraindication to IUD use. IUDs actually reduce overall pregnancy risk dramatically — and if a pregnancy does occur with an IUD in place, the risk of ectopic is relatively higher (but absolute pregnancy risk is very low).","IUD increases ectopic pregnancy risk in women with no prior ectopic","All IUDs are contraindicated — use implant only"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Prior ectopic pregnancy is NOT a contraindication to IUD use (WHO MEC Category 1). IUDs prevent pregnancy overall so effectively that even though intrauterine pregnancies are more effectively prevented than ectopic ones, the absolute risk of ectopic is still very low. The MOST important thing: if pregnancy occurs with IUD in place, evaluate for ectopic urgently. **Board tip:** Tubal factor infertility (damaged tubes) is NOT an IUD contraindication. The main contraindication to IUD: current cervical/uterine infection (not prior STI), unexplained vaginal bleeding, uterine anomaly that distorts cavity."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"Which contraceptive method is most appropriate for a woman who is breastfeeding and 4 weeks postpartum?","a":["Combined OCP — can start any time after delivery","Progestin-only methods (minipill, implant, LNG-IUD, DMPA) or copper IUD — progestin-only methods do not affect milk supply or infant","Combined patch or ring — same as OCP","Nothing — breastfeeding alone is sufficient contraception"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Postpartum <6 weeks breastfeeding: Combined estrogen-containing methods are WHO MEC Category 4 (absolute contraindication) — estrogen reduces milk supply and may pass through breast milk. Progestin-only methods are safe (do not affect lactation). DMPA can be given immediately postpartum. Implant/IUD can be inserted immediately postpartum (within 10 minutes of placenta delivery) or after 4 weeks. **Board tip:** Lactational Amenorrhea Method (LAM): effective only if FULLY breastfeeding, amenorrheic, and infant <6 months — then ~98% effective. Any of these conditions changing requires additional contraception."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A 42-year-old woman with migraine with aura asks about contraception. Which method is MOST appropriate?","a":["Combined OCP — effective and would help headaches","Progestin-only methods (minipill, implant, hormonal IUD) — migraine with aura is an absolute contraindication to estrogen-containing contraception due to increased stroke risk","Estrogen patch","Any combined hormonal method"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Migraine with aura + estrogen = WHO MEC Category 4 (absolute contraindication). Migraine with aura is associated with ischemic stroke risk; estrogen further increases this risk. Progestin-only methods are safe (MEC Category 1-2). Copper IUD is also safe. **Board tip:** Migraine WITHOUT aura + estrogen = MEC Category 2-3 (precaution, not absolute contraindication, but risks outweigh benefits for combined OCP — discuss alternatives). Migraine WITH aura = MEC 4 = never use estrogen-containing methods."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A patient is 3 days late starting her new pill pack (she missed 3 consecutive pills from a combined OCP). What is the correct guidance?","a":["Take all 3 missed pills at once and use backup for 2 days","Take the most recent missed pill, discard earlier missed pills, continue rest of pack with 7 days of backup contraception (condoms), and use emergency contraception if unprotected sex occurred in the past 5 days","Stop the pack and start a new one next month","Take all missed pills even if it causes nausea"],"correct":1,"rationale":"3+ missed combined OCP pills: Take most recently missed pill, discard earlier ones, continue pack, use backup (condoms) for 7 days. If unprotected sex occurred in the last 5 days: use EC. If fewer than 7 pills remain in the pack after the missed pills, skip the placebo week and start the next pack. **Board tip:** Missed pill rules: 1 missed pill = take as soon as remembered + continue, no backup needed. 2 missed pills = take 2 pills next 2 days + backup 7 days. 3+ missed = above protocol + consider EC."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"Which contraceptive method is indicated as BOTH a contraceptive AND treatment for primary dysmenorrhea and endometriosis-related pain?","a":["Copper IUD","Combined oral contraceptive pills, Depo-Provera, or LNG-IUD — progestogen-containing methods suppress endometrial growth and reduce prostaglandin-mediated pain","Condoms","Diaphragm"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Progestogen-containing methods (combined OCP, DMPA, LNG-IUD, etonogestrel implant) suppress endometrial proliferation, reduce prostaglandins, and provide significant pain relief for dysmenorrhea and endometriosis. LNG-IUD reduces endometriosis-related bleeding and pain. Combined OCP (continuous cycling) suppresses endometriosis most effectively. **Board tip:** First-line for endometriosis pain: NSAIDs AND hormonal contraception (combined OCP continuous or LNG-IUD). Second-line: GnRH agonists (with add-back HT). Definitive: surgery. The copper IUD WORSENS dysmenorrhea."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A 19-year-old chooses combined OCP for contraception. What is the most important counseling about drug interactions?","a":["OCP interacts with ALL vitamins","Enzyme-inducing medications (rifampin, certain antiepileptics like carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, and St. John's Wort) significantly reduce OCP efficacy — use backup contraception or choose alternative method","Only rifampin interacts with OCP","No medications interact with OCP"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Enzyme inducers that decrease OCP efficacy: Rifampin (strongest, 78% reduction in estrogen levels), antiepileptics (carbamazepine, phenytoin, oxcarbazepine, phenobarbital, topiramate), and St. John's Wort. With rifampin: use backup method for entire rifampin course + 28 days. With enzyme-inducing AEDs: use higher-dose OCP or alternative method (IUD, implant preferred). **Board tip:** Broad-spectrum antibiotics (amoxicillin, doxycycline) do NOT significantly reduce OCP efficacy despite popular belief — current evidence does not support the need for backup contraception with routine antibiotics (except rifampin)."},
  {"topic":"Contraception","course":"D120","q":"A patient requests female sterilization (tubal ligation). What should the counseling emphasize?","a":["100% effective — no failure rate possible","Intended as PERMANENT — reversal has low success rates. Failure rate ~0.5% lifetime (not zero). Ectopic pregnancy risk if failure. Should not be offered until patient is certain about permanent contraception.","Immediately reversible — no different from stopping the pill","Higher risk of cancer than IUD or implant"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Tubal ligation (or bilateral salpingectomy — preferred as it also reduces ovarian cancer risk): Permanent method with ~0.5% 10-year failure rate. Reversal possible but costly, not covered by insurance, and success rates decline with time and patient age. 1/3 of women who undergo sterilization later regret it — thorough counseling is essential. **Board tip:** Bilateral salpingectomy is now preferred over tubal ligation because it reduces ovarian cancer risk by ~60% (removes the tube where high-grade serous cancers initiate). Same contraceptive efficacy. Vasectomy for male partner is safer, cheaper, and more effective."},

  # ===== PRENATAL CARE (20) =====
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A 26-year-old G1P0 presents at 8 weeks gestation for her first prenatal visit. Which laboratory tests are standard initial prenatal labs?","a":["CBC, BMP, urine culture only","Blood type and Rh factor, antibody screen, CBC, rubella immunity, syphilis (RPR/VDRL), HIV, hepatitis B sAg, urine culture, gonorrhea/chlamydia, and Pap smear if due","Only blood type and CBC","Fasting glucose and thyroid panel only"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Standard first prenatal visit labs: Blood type and Rh factor (for RhoGAM planning), red cell antibody screen, CBC (anemia), rubella IgG (immunity status), syphilis (RPR), HIV (universal opt-out testing), Hepatitis B surface antigen, urine culture (screen for asymptomatic bacteriuria), gonorrhea/chlamydia NAAT, Pap smear/HPV co-test if due. **Board tip:** Additionally: Hep C antibody screening (USPSTF 2020), varicella immunity if history unknown, TB screen if high-risk, thyroid screening if symptoms. Tdap is given at 27-36 weeks (every pregnancy for passive immunity to newborn)."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A 30-year-old G2P1 is at 16 weeks gestation and asks about available prenatal genetic screening options. Which screening test evaluates risk for Down syndrome and neural tube defects?","a":["First trimester combined screen (nuchal translucency + PAPP-A + hCG) is only option","Quad screen (AFP, hCG, estriol, inhibin A) at 15-20 weeks screens for Down syndrome (T21), trisomy 18, and open neural tube defects","Amniocentesis is the only option for genetic screening","NIPT is only for women over 35"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Maternal serum quad screen (15-20 weeks): AFP (low = Down syndrome risk; high = NTD or ventral wall defect risk), hCG (elevated = Down syndrome), estriol (low = Down syndrome), inhibin A (elevated = Down syndrome). Detects ~80% of T21. **Board tip:** NIPT (cell-free DNA screening) is more sensitive and specific (>99% for T21) and is now offered to all pregnant patients regardless of age (not just AMA ≥35). NIPT screens for T21, T18, T13, sex chromosome abnormalities. Confirmatory diagnostic testing (amniocentesis or CVS) is needed for positive NIPT results."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"At what gestational age should a glucose challenge test (GCT) be performed to screen for gestational diabetes?","a":["First trimester (8-10 weeks) in all patients","24-28 weeks for all pregnant women — earlier if high risk (obesity, prior GDM, family history, PCOS)","36 weeks only","Only if patient gains >30 lbs during pregnancy"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Gestational diabetes (GDM) screening: GCT (1-hour 50g glucose challenge, no fasting required) at 24-28 weeks for all pregnancies. GCT ≥140 mg/dL → proceed to 3-hour 100g OGTT (diagnostic). ACOG/ACMG recommend early screening at first visit for high-risk women. **Board tip:** GDM diagnosis OGTT criteria (Carpenter-Coustan): fasting ≥95, 1-hr ≥180, 2-hr ≥155, 3-hr ≥140 mg/dL — 2+ values must be met. GDM increases risk of: macrosomia, shoulder dystocia, C-section, neonatal hypoglycemia, future T2DM for mother (50% within 10 years)."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A Rh-negative woman at 28 weeks gestation has no Rh antibodies. What is the standard of care?","a":["No intervention needed — Rh sensitization is uncommon","Administer Rho(D) immune globulin (RhoGAM) 300 mcg IM at 28 weeks, and again within 72 hours of delivery if baby is Rh positive","Test baby's blood type at 28 weeks before giving RhoGAM","Give RhoGAM only after delivery"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Rh-negative women with negative antibody screen receive RhoGAM at 28 weeks (antepartum prophylaxis) AND within 72 hours of delivery if baby is Rh positive. Also give RhoGAM after: miscarriage, abortion, ectopic pregnancy, amniocentesis, CVS, trauma, or antepartum hemorrhage. **Board tip:** Dose: 300 mcg IM covers 15 mL of fetal RBCs. If massive feto-maternal hemorrhage suspected (abruption, trauma): Kleihauer-Betke test determines dose needed. Goal: prevent maternal anti-D sensitization that causes hemolytic disease of the fetus/newborn in future pregnancies."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A 34-year-old woman at 12 weeks gestation asks about the benefits of folic acid supplementation. What is the recommended dose and timing?","a":["400 mcg starting at 12 weeks","400-800 mcg/day starting at least 1 month BEFORE conception and continuing through first trimester (400-800 mcg for general population; 4 mg for women with prior NTD-affected pregnancy or on antiepileptic drugs)","1000 mcg during the second trimester only","No folic acid needed if eating a balanced diet"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Folic acid (folate): Standard recommendation 400-800 mcg/day ideally starting 1-3 months BEFORE conception and continuing through 12 weeks (neural tube closure occurs at 28 days — before many women know they're pregnant). High-dose 4 mg for: prior NTD pregnancy, antiepileptic drugs (valproate especially), malabsorption. **Board tip:** Neural tube defects (spina bifida, anencephaly) occur at 28 days post-conception. By the time most women get prenatal care, the window for NTD prevention has passed — hence periconceptional supplementation."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"At what gestational age is the nuchal translucency (NT) ultrasound performed as part of first-trimester screening?","a":["6-8 weeks","11-14 weeks — measures fluid at the back of fetal neck; combined with PAPP-A and hCG for Down syndrome/trisomy 18 risk assessment","18-20 weeks anatomy scan","20-24 weeks"],"correct":1,"rationale":"First trimester combined screening: NT ultrasound (11 0/7 to 13 6/7 weeks) + maternal serum PAPP-A and free β-hCG → risk assessment for T21, T18, T13. NT >3mm is associated with chromosomal abnormalities and cardiac defects. **Board tip:** First trimester combined screen detects ~85-90% of Down syndrome (vs. ~80% for quad screen). Sequential screening (combining 1st and 2nd trimester screens) achieves 95%+ sensitivity. NIPT is preferred when available — higher sensitivity/specificity than serum biochemical screening."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A patient at 20 weeks presents with painless vaginal bleeding. Cervix is closed on exam. Ultrasound shows a low-lying placenta completely covering the internal os. What is the diagnosis and management?","a":["Placental abruption — emergency delivery","Placenta previa — pelvic rest, NO vaginal exams, repeat ultrasound at 32 weeks (many resolve), delivery plan (C-section mandatory if persists)","Bloody show of impending labor","Cervical polyp — remove in office"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Placenta previa: placenta covers internal cervical os. Presents as painless bright red vaginal bleeding in second/third trimester. NEVER perform digital vaginal exam (could precipitate massive hemorrhage). Management: pelvic rest, avoid intercourse/tampons, serial ultrasounds. Most low-lying placentas resolve as uterus grows. If persists at 32 weeks → plan C-section delivery at 36-37 weeks. **Board tip:** Distinguish: Placenta previa = painless bleeding. Placenta abruption = painful bleeding + uterine rigidity. Vasa previa = rupture of fetal vessels over os = fetal blood loss (fetal emergency)."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A 24-year-old at 36 weeks has BP 158/105, proteinuria 2+, and headache. No prior hypertension. What is the diagnosis and management?","a":["Chronic hypertension — increase antihypertensive medication","Preeclampsia with severe features — requires hospitalization, IV labetalol or hydralazine for acute BP control, IV magnesium sulfate for seizure prophylaxis, and delivery planning (≥37 weeks = deliver)","Gestational hypertension — observe at home","Anxiety-related blood pressure — reassure"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Preeclampsia with severe features (BP ≥160/110 on two occasions, severe-range symptoms: headache, visual changes, RUQ pain, pulmonary edema, platelets <100K, creatinine doubled): hospitalize, IV magnesium sulfate (eclampsia seizure prevention), IV antihypertensive for BP ≥160/110. Delivery at ≥37 weeks or earlier if severe/unstable. **Board tip:** Preeclampsia definition: new-onset hypertension (≥140/90) + proteinuria OR severe end-organ damage after 20 weeks. MgSO4 is the drug of choice for eclampsia prevention AND treatment. Give calcium gluconate as MgSO4 antidote if toxicity occurs (respiratory depression, loss of DTRs)."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"Which vaccine is recommended at every pregnancy, regardless of prior immunization history, at 27-36 weeks gestation?","a":["MMR","Tdap — provides passive immunity (anti-pertussis antibodies) to newborn through the placenta before birth, protecting the infant before their own vaccination at 2 months","Varicella","Hepatitis A"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Tdap is recommended at 27-36 weeks in EVERY pregnancy (not just first pregnancy) because placental antibody transfer peaks in third trimester — maternal vaccination protects the newborn in the first weeks of life before they can receive their own pertussis vaccination at 2 months. **Board tip:** Ideal timing: 27-32 weeks for maximum placental antibody transfer. Also recommended: influenza vaccine (any trimester), COVID-19 vaccine (updated formulation). Vaccines contraindicated in pregnancy: live vaccines (MMR, varicella, live-attenuated flu nasal spray). ONLY give inactivated influenza."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"Group B Streptococcus (GBS) screening is performed at which gestational age?","a":["First prenatal visit","35-37 weeks — vaginal/rectal culture; if positive, intrapartum IV penicillin during labor prevents neonatal GBS sepsis","At onset of labor only","20 weeks anatomy scan visit"],"correct":1,"rationale":"GBS screening: vaginal-rectal culture at 35-37 weeks. GBS-positive mothers receive IV penicillin (or ampicillin) during active labor (intrapartum antibiotic prophylaxis — IAP). GBS is a leading cause of neonatal early-onset sepsis. IAP reduces GBS neonatal infection by >80%. **Board tip:** IAP indications even without culture result: unknown GBS status + labor <37 weeks, ROM ≥18 hours, or intrapartum fever ≥100.4°F. Penicillin allergy: clindamycin (if susceptible) or cefazolin (if low anaphylaxis risk). Vancomycin if high-risk penicillin allergy."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A 32-year-old at 30 weeks presents with right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting, and elevated liver enzymes (AST 280, ALT 310), low platelets (88K), and hemolysis. BP is 150/95. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Cholecystitis — laparoscopic cholecystectomy","HELLP Syndrome (Hemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets) — a severe variant of preeclampsia requiring expedient delivery, IV MgSO4, and possibly steroids for fetal lung maturity if <34 weeks","Intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy — ursodiol only","Viral hepatitis — antiviral treatment"],"correct":1,"rationale":"HELLP Syndrome: Hemolysis (fragmented RBCs on smear, elevated LDH/bilirubin) + Elevated Liver enzymes + Low Platelets. A severe form of preeclampsia. Management: stabilize mother, IV MgSO4, antihypertensives, consider antenatal corticosteroids if <34 weeks, and expedite delivery. DIC can develop. **Board tip:** Mississippi Classification: Class 1 = platelets <50K (most severe), Class 2 = 50-100K, Class 3 = 100-150K. Tennessee Classification: complete vs. partial HELLP. Delivery is the only definitive treatment. Rarely recurs in subsequent pregnancies (<5%)."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A patient at 34 weeks gestation develops pruritus (severe itching without rash), particularly on the palms and soles at night. Liver function tests show elevated bile acids. What is the diagnosis and immediate concern?","a":["PUPP (Pruritic urticarial papules and plaques of pregnancy) — topical steroids","Intrahepatic Cholestasis of Pregnancy (ICP) — elevated bile acids (>10 μmol/L, severe >40) associated with increased risk of sudden fetal demise; treat with ursodiol, increase fetal monitoring, deliver at 37 weeks","Scabies — permethrin","Obstetric cholestasis is benign — reassure and moisturize"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Intrahepatic Cholestasis of Pregnancy: Elevated serum bile acids, intense pruritus (especially palms/soles), worse at night. RISK: sudden intrauterine fetal demise (mechanism not fully understood — possibly bile acid-induced fetal arrhythmia). Treatment: ursodiol (reduces bile acids, improves symptoms), twice-weekly NST, delivery at 37 weeks. **Board tip:** Bile acids >40 μmol/L = high risk of fetal demise. Laboratory: elevated bile acids, elevated AST/ALT, mildly elevated bilirubin. Distinguish from PUPP (rash within stretch marks, no liver involvement, no fetal risk)."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"What is the recommended weight gain during pregnancy for a woman with a normal pre-pregnancy BMI (18.5-24.9)?","a":["10-15 lbs total","25-35 lbs total, with approximately 1 lb/week in second and third trimesters","40-50 lbs","No weight gain guidelines — just eat healthy foods"],"correct":1,"rationale":"IOM recommended pregnancy weight gain: Underweight (BMI <18.5): 28-40 lbs; Normal (18.5-24.9): 25-35 lbs; Overweight (25-29.9): 15-25 lbs; Obese (≥30): 11-20 lbs. Inadequate gain: IUGR, preterm birth. Excessive gain: macrosomia, GDM, C-section, long-term maternal obesity. **Board tip:** Weight gain distribution: first trimester ~1-4 lbs total; second/third trimesters ~1 lb/week for normal BMI women. Components: fetus (~7-8 lbs), placenta/fluids/breast tissue/uterus expansion makes up the remainder."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A 28-year-old G1P0 at 11 weeks asks about chorionic villus sampling (CVS). How does CVS differ from amniocentesis?","a":["CVS and amniocentesis are interchangeable — no difference","CVS is performed at 10-13 weeks (earlier than amniocentesis at 15-20 weeks), samples placental tissue (not amniotic fluid), allows earlier diagnosis but cannot test for neural tube defects","CVS is safer than amniocentesis","Amniocentesis cannot detect chromosomal abnormalities"],"correct":1,"rationale":"CVS (10-13 weeks): samples chorionic villi (placental tissue) — provides chromosomal diagnosis 5-6 weeks earlier than amniocentesis. Cannot diagnose open NTDs (no amniotic AFP). Procedure-related loss rate: ~0.5-1%. Amniocentesis (15-20 weeks): samples amniotic fluid, can diagnose NTDs via AFP, slightly lower procedure risk (~0.1-0.3%). **Board tip:** Both are DIAGNOSTIC (not screening) procedures — they provide definitive chromosomal diagnosis. Offered to all patients (not just AMA). Transplacental CVS → avoid Rh-negative mothers if possible (or give RhoGAM)."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"Which nutrient supplementation is specifically recommended in the third trimester and while breastfeeding, particularly for vegetarians and those with limited dairy intake?","a":["Iron 325 mg three times daily","Vitamin D 600 IU/day and calcium 1000 mg/day — fetal bone mineralization peaks in third trimester; breastfeeding depletes maternal calcium","Vitamin A megadose","Zinc supplements daily"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Calcium and Vitamin D: critical for fetal bone development (third trimester most critical). Recommended intake: calcium 1000 mg/day, Vitamin D 600 IU/day (higher doses may be needed if deficient). Breastfeeding: maternal bone resorption of 3-5% occurs to supply breast milk calcium — recovers after weaning. **Board tip:** Iron supplementation: 27 mg/day prenatal (more if anemic). DHA/omega-3: 200mg/day from week 20 (brain and retinal development). Iodine: 220 mcg/day (important for thyroid/fetal brain — many prenatal vitamins are inadequate). Avoid vitamin A megadose (>10,000 IU): teratogenic."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A pregnant patient at 28 weeks tests positive for asymptomatic bacteriuria (ASB) with >100,000 CFU/mL of E. coli. How should this be managed?","a":["Treat only if symptoms develop","Treat immediately with appropriate antibiotics (nitrofurantoin, cephalexin, or amoxicillin-clavulanate) — ASB in pregnancy MUST be treated to prevent progression to pyelonephritis (which increases preterm labor risk)","Annual reculture — no treatment","Repeat culture in 4 weeks before treating"],"correct":1,"rationale":"Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy: ALWAYS treat (unlike non-pregnant women). Untreated ASB in pregnancy progresses to pyelonephritis in 30-40% of cases, which is associated with preterm labor and low birth weight. Preferred antibiotics: nitrofurantoin (avoid at term/≥36 weeks), cephalexin, or amoxicillin-clavulanate. Test of cure culture 1-2 weeks after treatment. **Board tip:** Screen for ASB with urine culture at first prenatal visit. Monthly dipstick alone is insufficient — culture is required. Avoid fluoroquinolones in pregnancy. Avoid TMP-SMX in first trimester (folate antagonism) and near term."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"A 29-year-old at 20 weeks has an anatomy ultrasound. Which fetal structural abnormality cannot be detected on the 20-week anatomy scan?","a":["Anencephaly","Neural tube defects","Structural heart defects (major)","Phenylketonuria (metabolic disorder — requires biochemical testing, not ultrasound)"],"correct":3,"rationale":"Anatomy ultrasound at 18-20 weeks evaluates: fetal anatomy (brain, spine, heart, abdominal organs, face, limbs), placental location, amniotic fluid volume, cervical length. It CANNOT diagnose metabolic/biochemical disorders (PKU, cystic fibrosis, inborn errors of metabolism) — these require genetic testing (amniocentesis, newborn screening). **Board tip:** The 20-week anatomy scan is the single most important ultrasound of pregnancy. It detects major structural anomalies. Fetal echocardiography (done at 18-24 weeks) is recommended for high-risk pregnancies (maternal diabetes, CHD family history, abnormal screening)."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"What is the FIRST-LINE treatment for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy (morning sickness) in the first trimester?","a":["Ondansetron (Zofran) immediately","Dietary modifications (small frequent meals, avoid triggers, ginger) + vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) ± doxylamine (Unisom) — combination is FDA-approved (Diclegis/Bonjesta)","Promethazine suppositories first-line","Metoclopramide (Reglan) first-line"],"correct":1,"rationale":"NVP management ladder: (1) Dietary modifications + vitamin B6 10-25mg TID; (2) Add doxylamine 12.5mg (Unisom SleepTabs half tablet) — the B6+doxylamine combination is FDA Pregnancy Category A (Diclegis/Bonjesta approved); (3) Add antihistamines, promethazine, metoclopramide; (4) Ondansetron (Category B — limited safety data). **Board tip:** Hyperemesis gravidarum (>5% weight loss, dehydration, electrolyte abnormalities) requires IV fluids and IV antiemetics. Rule out thyroid storm, UTI, molar pregnancy. Ondansetron: avoid if possible in first trimester (possible QTc and cleft concerns, limited data)."},
  {"topic":"Prenatal","course":"D120","q":"What is the indication for low-dose aspirin (81 mg/day) initiated at 12-16 weeks of pregnancy?","a":["All pregnant women to prevent miscarriage","Women at high risk for preeclampsia (prior preeclampsia, chronic HTN, diabetes, renal disease, autoimmune disease, multifetal gestation, first pregnancy, Black race, BMI >30, age >35) — reduces risk of preeclampsia by 10-24%","Prevent DVT in pregnancy","Prevent gestational diabetes"],"correct":1,"rationale":"USPSTF recommends low-dose aspirin (81 mg/day) initiated at 12-16 weeks (before 16 weeks for maximal benefit) for women with ≥1 high-risk factor or ≥2 moderate-risk factors for preeclampsia. It inhibits thromboxane A2 production and improves uteroplacental blood flow. **Board tip:** ASPRE trial and systematic reviews confirm 10-24% reduction in preterm preeclampsia with low-dose aspirin. Continue until delivery. Also used for antiphospholipid syndrome in pregnancy (with heparin). High-risk factors: prior preeclampsia, multifetal gestation, CKD, autoimmune, diabetes, HTN."},
]

